  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. While there is limited documentation on specific styles popular during this time period, we can infer that protective hairstyles, such as those designed to reduce breakage and exposure to environmental elements, were likely valued by women of African descent. The afro-textured hair of many Black and African individuals would have been a defining feature, with some opting for the natural growth of their curls or using chemical curling products to style them into distinctive patterns. Cornrows, a traditional hairstyle in many African cultures, may also have been popular among women during this era. Unfortunately, due to the limited source material available, we cannot provide more specific details on the hairstyles and hair culture of Black and African women during the 1930s.",
